# iOS Data And Network Standards

## Purpose

Define how the existing iOS project should call backend services, transform data, handle errors, and expose results to the rest of the app.

## Current Project Pattern

- Networking code lives under `App/DailyWire/Services/Networking`.
- `BaseClient`, `SessionManager`, `RequestRetryExecutor`, and `APIError` own shared transport behavior.
- Middleware endpoint code lives under `Services/Networking/Middleware`.
- Middleware DTOs and response models live under `Services/Networking/Middleware/Models`.
- `Middleware.Client` is registered in Swinject and also exposed behind protocols such as `MiddlewareClient`, `MediaNetworkService`, and `AppSettingsNetworkService`.
- Feature services/providers such as live content, continue watching, follow hosts, EPG, Shorts, and CarPlay consume middleware and map into app behavior.
- Mock middleware responses and stubs exist for deterministic tests.

```text
App/DailyWire/Services/Networking/
├── BaseClient/
│   ├── BaseClient.swift
│   ├── APIError.swift
│   ├── RequestRetryExecutor.swift
│   └── SessionManager.swift
├── Middleware/
│   ├── Middleware+Client.swift
│   ├── MiddlewareClient+Protocol.swift
│   ├── MiddlewareClientStub.swift
│   ├── Models/
│   └── MockResponses/
└── LiveChatAPI/
```

## Required Standards

- Shared transport behavior belongs in networking clients or helpers, not in feature view models or views.
- Middleware DTOs represent wire format and must not leak into UI when nullability, entitlement, formatting, or availability needs normalization.
- Feature services/providers expose app-level behavior and return domain or UI-ready models, explicit state, or typed errors.
- Mappers convert DTOs to domain/UI models at the data boundary.
- Network errors, parsing errors, HTTP errors, authentication errors, connectivity errors, and unsupported-content cases must be represented explicitly where callers need to respond differently.
- Treat force-refresh behavior as an explicit input.
- Centralize cache, session, token, and environment behavior in networking clients or services.
- Avoid hardcoded endpoint values, environment selectors, API keys, tokens, user identifiers, or secrets in feature code and standards examples.
- Do not add new networking libraries unless the existing stack cannot safely support the requirement.

## Error And Privacy Boundaries

- Map transport/backend errors into feature-level errors before UI handling.
- Do not display raw backend, transport, or decoding error strings as user-facing copy.
- Do not log secrets, tokens, session IDs, user identifiers, entitlement payloads, or raw response bodies.
- Do not pass raw middleware DTOs into analytics.

## Preferred Flow

Request path:

```text
ViewModel / Coordinator
-> Service / Provider
-> MiddlewareClient protocol or Middleware.Client
-> BaseClient / SessionManager
-> Backend
```

Response path:

```text
DTO / response
-> mapper
-> domain or UI-ready model
-> explicit page state / result
-> ViewModel UI state
```

## Do / Don't

Do:

```swift
final class FeatureProvider {
    private let middlewareClient: MiddlewareClient
    private let mapper: FeatureMapper

    init(middlewareClient: MiddlewareClient, mapper: FeatureMapper) {
        self.middlewareClient = middlewareClient
        self.mapper = mapper
    }

    func page(slug: String, membershipPlan: String?, isForced: Bool) async throws -> FeaturePage {
        let response = try await middlewareClient.getPostPage(
            id: .slug(slug),
            membershipPlan: membershipPlan,
            isForced: isForced
        )
        return mapper.map(response)
    }
}
```

Don't:

```swift
@MainActor
final class FeatureViewModel: ObservableObject {
    private let client: Middleware.Client

    func load() async throws {
        uiState.items = try await client.getPostPage(
            id: .slug("feature"),
            membershipPlan: nil,
            isForced: false
        ).components
    }
}
```

Reason: view models should depend on app behavior, not transport details or wire shape.

Do:

```swift
struct FeatureMapper {
    func map(_ item: Middleware.ComponentItem) -> FeatureItem? {
        guard let title = item.title, !title.isEmpty else {
            return nil
        }

        return FeatureItem(id: item.id, title: title)
    }
}
```

Don't:

```swift
struct FeatureRow: View {
    let item: Middleware.ComponentItem

    var body: some View {
        Text(item.title ?? "")
    }
}
```

Reason: DTO nullability and wire quirks should be normalized before UI.

## Caching And Refresh

- Treat refresh as an explicit argument such as `isForced` or `forceRefresh`.
- Preserve offline behavior when changing caching, persistence, or refresh logic.
- Do not let UI components know HTTP cache headers, token refresh behavior, or session ID wiring.
- Clear or refresh caches on auth/session changes when cached data depends on entitlement or user identity.

## Exceptions

- A very small provider can return DTOs internally if the caller immediately maps them before exposing data to UI.
- Existing code may mix service and client responsibilities. New code should keep layers separate.
- Real-backend checks are allowed when contract confidence is the purpose, but automated feature tests should prefer deterministic mocked responses.

## Validation Expectations

- For new endpoints or request options, test success and representative failure cases.
- For mappers, test null, missing, entitlement, and variant fields when middleware contracts permit them.
- For cache or force-refresh changes, test both cached and refresh paths.
- For authentication-sensitive requests, verify session/token behavior without logging secrets or user identifiers.
